statementMd:
You are given 2 qubits which are guaranteed to be in one of the four orthogonal states:

- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/49e51a6f9be9e5bedeabaa7ca5e7bd92dcc1ab5d.png)
- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/95e233a39166d0267c91286fe4cf12ae7304c729.png)
- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/7879ef981f7a9e5d0558b430f38eb6d9948ac4cd.png)
- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/a982638d2f4b65b45c549e6cd5dad0b1a647e8dc.png)

Your task is to perform necessary operations and measurements to figure out which state it was and to return the index of that state (0 for ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/afa7acae20d2fab7a9f92e874117fe5110a64bec.png), 1 for ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/af50cdeb431e258f308aab43ec055f87a0fd8827.png) etc.). The state of the qubits after the operations does not matter.

You have to implement an operation which takes an array of 2 qubits as an input and returns an integer.

Your code should have the following signature:

```
namespace Solution {
    open Microsoft.Quantum.Primitive;
    open Microsoft.Quantum.Canon;

    operation Solve (qs : Qubit[]) : Int
    {
        body
        {
            // your code here
        }
    }
}
```

